              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The most efficient and recommended way to change the color of a `<span>` on hover is using **CSS**. However, you can also do this with **JavaScript** if you need more dynamic control.\n\nHere are the best ways to achieve this:\n\n---\n\n### Method 1: CSS (Recommended)\nThis is the cleanest and most performance-friendly method. It uses the CSS `:hover` pseudo-class.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n    <style>\n        /* Define the default state of the span */\n        .color-change-span {\n            color: black; /* Original color */\n            cursor: pointer; /* Changes cursor to a pointer (optional) */\n            transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Smooth transition effect (optional) */\n        }\n\n        /* Define what happens when the mouse is over the span */\n        .color-change-span:hover {\n            color: #007bff; /* Color on hover (blue) */\n        }\n    </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n\n    <p>This is a paragraph with a <span class=\"color-change-span\">hoverable text span</span> inside it.</p>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 2: Inline JavaScript (Quick & Simple)\nIf you need a quick fix directly in your HTML without a separate CSS file or `<style>` block, you can use the inline `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes.\n\n```html\n<span \n    style=\"color: black; cursor: pointer;\" \n    onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red'\" \n    onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black'\">\n    Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n\n---\n\n### Method 3: JavaScript Event Listeners (For Dynamic Logic)\nIf you are building an interactive web app and need to handle hover states dynamically using JavaScript, you can attach event listeners to the element.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<body>\n\n    <span id=\"my-span\" style=\"color: black; cursor: pointer;\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n        const span = document.getElementById('my-span');\n\n        // Change color when mouse enters\n        span.addEventListener('mouseenter', () => {\n            span.style.color = 'green';\n        });\n\n        // Revert color when mouse leaves\n        span.addEventListener('mouseleave', () => {\n            span.style.color = 'black';\n        });\n    </script>\n\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### Which one should you use?\n* **Use CSS (Method 1)** for 99% of use cases. It keeps your HTML and styling separate and is highly optimized by browsers.\n* **Use JavaScript (Method 2 or 3)** only if the color needs to change dynamically based on user data, state, or complex logic.",
